speechtext:
My hon. friend is quite correct if we were following strictly legal practice. But just let- pie recall the procedure which is now being followed in awarding these pensions. The claim comes in, with the evidence of the physical condition of the applicant upon the investigation made by the Army Medical Service before the man was discharged. Then there is the information gathered from the man himself before discharge. The information comes down to the Board of Pension Commissioners.' If it is complete, and there is no apparent departure from the practice, and it appears to be a reasonably clear case, then, after being passed upon by the medical branch of the Pensions Board, the degree of disability is estimated, and the report is made to the Pension Board, as such. Then one of the commissioners, as a matter of ordinary routine, passes upon that application, and if satisfied that everything is regular he would approve it and the pension would be granted. The number of cases of contest would be very few indeed. The pension is granted as a matter of routine upon certain evidence gathered under the regulations passed by the Pensions Board and as the law requires. If the pension is so awarded, and the applicant is dissatisfied, then he may appeal. I used the word "appeal" not in the strictly legal sense
[Mr. I .ernieux.}
in which my hon. friend used it, but in the sense that the case might come before the full board to reverse or change the former decision upon the same evidence or with evidence if the applicant so desired. We could use the word "review" there, and I think it would mean the substantially same thing. The establishing of an independent pension court of appeal was discussed before the Pensions Committee. Under certain conditions something might be said in favour of it; but it was thought that that would be creating too complicated machinery, and that perhaps the expense would not be justified. On the whole it was thought wiser to still leave the final authority in the board just as it has been for the past two or three years. If we find difficulty in working the matter out, it can be considered in the future. I appreciate the importance of the preint raised by my hon. friend, and as I say, it was considered by the Pensions Committee, and they reached the conclusion that I have mentioned.
